i'm dustin ingram developer advocate for  Google cloud platform I'm really excited  to announce the general availability of  Python 3 runtime on the App Engine  standard environment Python is one of  the most popular programming languages  and growing it's well suited for a  number of use cases including web  applications machine learning and Big  Data App Engine has supported a Python  runtime from the very beginning more  than 10 years now you may have tried  Python on App Engine in the past and  notice that it was very opinionated  about what you could and couldn't do  this new Python runtime is very  different Python 37 on App Engine  is our second-generation runtime that  means it's based on open source Python  from the Python Software Foundation it  supports Google cloud client libraries  for services like cloud storage vision  translate etc you can install libraries  frameworks and other dependencies using  requirements techs and best of all  Python 3 I like to deploy web apps to  App Engine because my app scales  automatically up or down even down to  zero to meet incoming traffic spikes I  only need to think about my Python code  not the infrastructure or wiring that  runs my app and I only pay for what my  app uses and I can use App engines free  tier to easily try new things in this  video I'll show you how to use flask a  popular and versatile web framework to  quickly write deploy and test a Python 3  web app on App Engine once we've got the  app deployed we'll iterate on it a bit  to take advantage of some of those great  new features there are three files that  will make up our app app Gamal main Pi  and requirements text let's start with  app Gamal this file lets you configure  your app in a number of different ways  all we need to do in this file is tell  App Engine that we want to use the new  Python 37 runtime we do this by adding  these lines next moving down our list of  files let's define our apps behavior by  creating our main PI file this is where  we can define the core logic of our  application we'll call it main PI  because that's what App Engine uses by  default but you can call  whatever you want as long as you can  figure it appropriately we'll use flask  as our web framework but you can use any  framework you'd like as well by default  App Engine looks for a variable called  app inside your main dot PI file as you  might guess this should correspond to  your app we'll define an endpoint at the  root URL that just says hello world and  that's it finally the new Python runtime  understands the same requirements format  that is used by PIP and other package  installers and runtimes across the  entire Python ecosystem the requirements  text file because we've chosen flask as  our web framework will specify it as a  dependency in a file called requirements  text will add flask and pin it's version  to be exactly version 1.0 point to the  latest version that's currently  available pinning the version of a  dependency ensures that the next time  you deploy your app you won't install a  newer and possibly incompatible version  of your dependency which could lead to  breakage so now we've defined these  three files optimal main PI and  requirements dot text our app is  finished let's deploy it deploying is  easy with the g-cloud command line tool  once you've installed it and  authenticated yourself all you need to  do is type g-cloud app deploy to deploy  your app to App Engine  now that it's done we can type g-cloud  app browse to view the app looks great  now let's see what else we can do web  apps are usually most useful when they  can accept input as well as produce  output what if we just want to say hello  to a specific person let's see how we  can do this with flask by importing the  request object we can inspect the query  string parameters from the URL we'll  look for one called name let's deploy  that real quick with g-cloud app deploy  if we don't pass any parameters it stays  the same and says hi to the whole world  but if we add a name equals Python 3  parameter to the end of the URL we've  now passed that value into our app and  it's rendered as you would expect since  this is Python 3 we can make our code  even better by using f strings instead  of string concatenation or by sticking a  few emojis right in  our source code putting the emoji in  line like that is great but what if we  wanted the user to be able to include an  emoji of their own choice by specifying  it as text we could create a massive  mapping from emoji names to their actual  values and find and replace them in our  name parameter instead let's use this  third party emoji package which is open  source and available on the Python  package index this package provides an  emoji eyes function which will let us  turn text into emojis all we need to do  is add the dependency to our  requirements text file like so remember  always pin your dependencies now in main  PI we can import the emojis function  from the emoji library and then use it  to emoji eyes the entire greeting once  again we'll deploy it with g-cloud app  deploy now let's add the string : snake  : to our name parameter and see what it  looks like in our browser it looks great  so much more customizable so that's all  there is to making a Python 3 app that  handles user input and uses third-party  libraries from pi pi stay tuned for the  next episode where i'll show you how to  deploy a wagtail app to App Engine  feel free to ask questions in the  comments below and if you liked this  video please subscribe to the GCP  channel for more videos like this thanks  for watching  [Music]
 

